# Epic Structure Guidelines

When analyzing or creating Epics in the project, verify that all required fields are present and help fill them in as needed.

## 1. **Background**
> Provides business and technical context.  
> Explains **why** this task is needed — include purpose, impact, or dependencies.

## 2. **Requirement**
> A clear and specific description of **what needs to be implemented**.  
> Focus on system behavior, logic, and expected user interaction.

## 3. **List of Brands to Be Rolled Out**
> Specify the affected brands — e.g. "All brands" (all supported brands) or a specific list (RC UK, RC AU, etc.).  
> "All brands" is sufficient; listing each brand is not required.

## 4. **Links**
> Include all relevant references:  
> – Figma mockups  
> – Technical documentation  
> – API specifications  
> – Related Jira tickets or Confluence pages  

## 5. **Rollout Dates**
> Provide the rollout date to prod (when the change will be deployed).  
> A single date is sufficient; Start–End range and phases are optional.

## 6. **Acceptance Criteria**
> A bullet-point checklist defining the **conditions for completion**.  
> Each point should be testable and written clearly.

**Example:**
- [ ] User can access the new field on both mobile and desktop
- [ ] Input is validated against backend constraints
- [ ] Analytics event is triggered and tracked correctly

---

**Assistant actions:**  
When reviewing an Epic in Jira/Confluence or creating a new Epic, verify that all six sections are present; if anything is missing, suggest wording or content according to this structure.
